WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.427
Temporary file security in Python means treating short-lived storage as
real storage with confidentiality, integrity, availability, and
auditability risks.

2
00:00:09.427 --> 00:00:20.575
Temporary files appear in uploads, reports, archives, image processing,
subprocess workflows, tests, data pipelines, background jobs, containers,
serverless runtimes, and local tools.

3
00:00:20.575 --> 00:00:27.043
They may live briefly, but while they exist they can contain sensitive
data or influence important processing.

4
00:00:27.043 --> 00:00:41.081
Strong designs use vetted temporary file APIs, generated unpredictable
names, controlled directories, safe permissions, path-aware handling,
containment checks where appropriate, least-privilege processing, and
reliable cleanup.

5
00:00:41.081 --> 00:00:54.568
They avoid direct user control over temporary paths, separate display
names from storage names, minimize sensitive data written to disk, and
avoid secrets or private context in filenames, logs, crash reports, and
support bundles.

6
00:00:54.568 --> 00:00:57.252
Temporary file safety is a workflow property.

7
00:00:57.252 --> 00:01:09.225
A secure creation call is not enough if later steps pass the file to a
subprocess unsafely, extract archives into the wrong location, leave
partial outputs after failure, or log sensitive paths and contents.

8
00:01:09.225 --> 00:01:16.382
The full pipeline should be reviewed from input through processing,
output, cleanup, monitoring, and incident response.

9
00:01:16.382 --> 00:01:26.773
The goal is predictable, authorized temporary storage that supports
processing needs without leaking data, weakening file boundaries, or
leaving sensitive artifacts behind.

10
00:01:26.773 --> 00:01:36.200
When teams design temporary storage deliberately, they reduce subtle risks
and make Python applications easier to operate, review, and recover after
failures.
